.TH UTIME 2
.SH NAME
utime \- set file times
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "#include <sys/types.h>"
.B "utime(file, timep)"
.B "char *file;"
.B "time_t timep[2];"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B utime
call uses the `accessed' and `updated' times in that order from
the
.I timep
vector to set the corresponding recorded times for
.IR file .
.PP
The caller must be the owner of the file or the super-user.
.PP
Actually, the `accessed' time is always set to the current time.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
stat(2)
.SH ASSEMBLER
(utime = 30.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
sys utime; file; timep
.fi
.TP
8086:
.nf
BX = 30
AX = file
DX = timep
int 0Xf1
.fi
.sp
